# Always check Ubuntu / Linux permissions

Before you finish any change that touches **PHP**, **static assets**, **patches**, **Hostinger drops**, or **release zips**, check permissions the way Ubuntu Apache (`www-data`) sees them. Do not skip this because the files look fine on the agent VM.

## Required layout

| Path | Mode | Why |
|------|------|-----|
| Directories | `755` | Apache must traverse (`+x`) or the site is **403** |
| PHP / JS / CSS / images | `644` | Apache must read; Hostinger **500s** on world-writable (`777`) PHP |
| Shell scripts (`*.sh`) | `755` | `sudo bash fix-ubuntu-perms.sh` |
| `data/` | `775` | SQLite + WAL; `755` owned by `ubuntu` → HTTP **500** |
| `data/*.db` | `664` | www-data must write |
| `.env` | `640` | readable by the app user, not world-readable |
| Document root | **not** `700` | `umask 077 mkdir` → **403 Forbidden** |

**Never** “fix” Ubuntu with `chmod -R 777`.

## What to run

```bash
bash scripts/check-ubuntu-perms.sh
```

That script must pass. It checks the working tree, the invoice logo (`static/img/inpmnt-logo-invoice.jpg` world-readable), and `patches/inpmnt-hostinger-changed.tar.gz` (dirs `755`, files `644`, scripts `755`).

On a real Ubuntu Apache docroot after unzip:

```bash
sudo bash /var/www/html/fix-ubuntu-perms.sh
```

## Packaging

- Hostinger tarballs: `umask 022`, `chmod` 755/644 (then `755` on `*.sh`), `tar --numeric-owner --owner=0 --group=0`.
- After regenerating patches, re-run `scripts/check-ubuntu-perms.sh`.
- Windows `Compress-Archive` zips can extract as `777` or unreadable on Ubuntu — do not ship those without a Unix-mode packer or the fix script.
